  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=3^x+2$ and $g(x)=x^2+2x+3$. Find the range of",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p22-q9a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f\\circ g$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$[11,\\infty)$.\n\nThe minimum occurs at $x=-1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}g(x)&=(x+1)^2+2,\\\\g(x)&\\ge2,\\\\(f\\circ g)(x)&=3^{g(x)}+2,\\\\(f\\circ g)(x)&\\ge3^2+2=11.\\end{align*}$$\nEquality holds at $x=-1$. Since $g$ is unbounded above and $f$ is increasing, the range is $[11,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q9b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$g\\circ f$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(11,\\infty)$.\n\nThe inner function takes every value greater than $2$.",
          "worked": "The range of $f$ is $(2,\\infty)$. Put $t=f(x)$, so $t>2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}g(t)&=(t+1)^2+2,\\\\t+1&>3,\\\\g(t)&>11.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $t>2$, $g(t)$ increases from the excluded limit $11$ without an upper bound. The range is $(11,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      }
    ]
  },
